# Round 19 (writer 10): settled; was REFCORE-UNSPECIFIED E §3.2. GD-22: E §3.2 "A stretch of law dates over which the rows and rules the item reaches don't change is one class
# even where no row covers it, so a window at inside such a stretch is read once, at its first day inside the window,
# and gives one hole, which names that day": q3 is the gap hole of 2026-06-10. The old comment:
# Round 18, C3-7. As L4_3_at_window_lifespan with the window inside ola's life, 2026-06-10 .. 2026-06-14, and no row of
# city: each day gives the gap rule's hole naming that day (L §4.6 step 5), while E §3.2 says every law date of a class
# gives one value, and whether the window is one class (one hole, at its first day, L §4.3) or several turns on the
# class's unstated extent: REFCORE-UNSPECIFIED (snap11 merged the five questions).
=== m.tally
model t
  tally 2
  on 2026-12-31
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2026-12-31

source s "s"
kind persons
input city[p in persons]: text
input w: date

def c[p in persons]: text at w
  rule r = city[p]

version v
  cite s
  governs always
  known 2026-01-01
  persons + [ola] lives 1950-01-01..2026-06-15
  w = window(2026-06-10, 2026-06-14)

ask q3: c[ola]
=== expect
q3: ?"city at ola: no value at 2026-06-10" (city[ola])
  cut 2026-12-31
